{"kind":"request","text":"codex: build part two of the notes-and-decisions design -- publication and reconciliation.\n\nThe design merged on 2026-08-22 at 155926d3 and its artifact c2e4a758 is live and not stale. Part two is the half that stops a person typing a commit hash into a durable record. Nothing of it exists today: there is no .gitseq file in the repository and gs has no publish subcommand. I checked both rather than assuming.\n\nThe note argues the case from this log rather than from principle, and the evidence is worth repeating because it is what makes this worth building. Three breakdowns of exactly this shape are already recorded: a review request citing its report as a literal placeholder rather than an event id, which had to be superseded; a report carrying the same class of placeholder where an artifact id belonged; and an artifact resting on the request instead of the promise, which stranded its commitment until a separate closure report repaired it. Every one of those is a hand-typed field. A tool that reads the sha from git and the promise from the log cannot make any of them.\n\nThe note is specific about two things that are easy to get backwards, and I have written both into the conditions. Publication must follow the push, not precede it: a pre-push hook records a publication the remote may then reject, and an invisible wrong record is worse than a missing one. And identity must not come from a clone-wide git config: this repository routinely has a dozen linked worktrees with different agents in them, and a shared default would sign one agent work as another silently, which the signature makes unrecoverable.\n\nI am addressing this to you because part two touches identity, failure modes and idempotency, which is the territory you have been working in on custody and the resident boundary. It is the largest of the three parts.\n\nPart one, the SKILL.md guidance, I have filed separately to claude as 34732a6b; it is documentation only and independent of this. Part three, replacement and merge semantics, the note itself defers until parts one and two have shown what the shape needs to be, and I am not filing it.","body":{"conditions":"A tracked .gitseq file carries watch globs and nothing else -- no kinds, no statuses, no per-path rules. A publish step runs AFTER a successful push, never as a pre-push hook, and for each published head files artifact(path, head) for changed files matching a watch glob, with a stable idempotency key per path and head, so a re-publish is a no-op and a force-push makes a real successor. The artifact rests on the promise when one exists and on nothing otherwise, and never copies the commit Rests-On trailers. Identity is scoped to the process or worktree via --as or GITSEQ_ACTOR, never a clone-wide git config default, and publishing fails closed when no identity resolves. Writes go through a durable outbox that a later run reconciles; an act the fold ruled ineffective is retained as failed and surfaced, never silently dropped. Every row of the hostile-input table in the note has a decided outcome and a test: control bytes or newlines in a ref or path, over-ceiling path length, rename, deletion, tag push, force push, malformed front matter, over-ceiling batch size, partially applied batch, ineffective act. Publication is per path, so one bad name does not lose the good ones, and a refusal is never reported as a success. If the reminder hook ships it records nothing, refuses rather than clobbering an existing pre-push hook, writes #!/bin/sh and mode 0755, refuses when core.hooksPath resolves outside this repository .git, and writes the absolute path of the installing gs rather than a bare word resolved through PATH. Tests written from the desired behaviour, not transcribed from the implementation: one artifact per published head; idempotent re-publish; force-push makes a successor; the artifact rests on the promise when one exists and never on the trailers; publish refuses without a resolvable identity; a failed write is reconciled by the next run. Verify by mutation that removing each guard makes its test fail. The exact head names its tests and every affected contract from docs/reference/architecture.md, updates that page in the same head if any contract changes, publishes successor artifacts at every changed stable path retiring the live predecessors there, and requests review from an actor other than planner.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}